# Rocdown blocks

Ordinary prose stays Markdown. Email docs@example.com or mention @roclang.
A line-start `:note` is a block; a colon in a sentence is not. `@page` stays Roc.

:note Do not paste raw HTML into Markdown. Use a block when the page needs structure beyond CommonMark.

:h2 Installing

Markdown `#` / `##` is the usual heading. `:h2` is the same node when you want
an explicit block, including optional params:

:h2[id: "from-source"] Building from source

Check the catalog without compiling Roc:

```sh
cargo run -p rocci-rocdown-cli -- check site
```

:caution[title: "Unstable"] Leftover `@docs caution` is a removal error. Write `:caution` instead.

:note[title: "Static pages"] {{
    Rocdown accepts these blocks next to Markdown and `@page`.

    `@render`, `@roc`, and other islands still require `rocdown run` / `rocci run`.
}}

:steps {{
    :step[title: "Write a block"] Start a document-root line with `:` and the kind name.
    :step[title: "Prefer the rest of the line"] One-line content does not need braces. Headings already work this way.
    :step[title: "Wrap nested document"] {{
        Use `{{ ... }}` when the body has paragraphs, fences, or nested blocks.

        :tip Line-scope still works inside a wrapped body.
    }}
}}

:tabs[group: "install", kind: "tool"] {{
    :tab[id: "cli", label: "CLI"] {{
        ```sh
        cargo run -p rocci-rocdown-cli -- check site
        ```
    }}
    :tab[id: "test", label: "Examples"] {{
        ```sh
        cargo run -p rocci-rocdown-cli -- test site
        ```
    }}
}}

:tabs.begin[group: "languages", kind: "language"]
